# Frontend environment — copy to .env.local (gitignored).
# Every variable below is read somewhere under apps/frontend; nothing here is
# aspirational. Values are deliberately absent: this file names the knobs, the
# real values live in Vercel's project settings.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- API

# The content API's base URL, read on the server: lib/api.ts (all page content)
# and next.config.ts, which derives the allowed next/image host from it — an
# uploaded photo will not render if this is wrong. Required in production; see
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL below. `next dev` defaults to http://localhost:3333/api.
# API_URL=

# The same origin, read in the browser: lib/uploads.ts (patient file upload),
# lib/leads.ts (contact, EXPRESS and deliverable forms) and lib/newsletter.ts
# (the footer signup and the library's email gate). Must be publicly reachable.
#
# Both of these are REQUIRED for a production build (audit A7). The old default
# was http://localhost:3333/api in four separate places, which in production is
# a site that reads no content and forms that post to the visitor's own
# machine; `lib/api-base.ts` throws instead. `next dev` still defaults.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=

# The site's own public origin, no trailing slash. robots.txt, sitemap.xml and
# every canonical/hreflang link need absolute URLs, so this is what they are
# built from. On Vercel it falls back to VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL, which
# points at production even from a preview deployment; set this explicitly once
# the real domain is delegated.
#
# REQUIRED for a production build. With neither this nor the Vercel variable
# set, `next build` fails rather than shipping a robots.txt that points a
# crawler at localhost (audit A7, F6). `next dev` still defaults to
# http://localhost:3000, which is where the site actually is.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Booking
#
# Nothing to set. Every Calendly link, the free consultation included, comes
# from the service catalog and is edited in the back office. The built-in
# fallbacks pointed at the developer's test account and went with audit A7: a
# service with no link renders no booking button, and the standing "Programează"
# button in the header, the hero and the footer navigates to /services instead.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Analytics

# All four are optional and independent; with none of them set the analytics
# stack is a no-op and no third-party script is ever loaded. Consent comes from
# the self-hosted banner (CookieConsent v3): GTM and GA4 wait for the analytics
# category, the Pixel for marketing.

# Google Tag Manager container — the recommended umbrella, manage GA4 and the
# Pixel inside it rather than setting them here as well.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GTM_ID=

# Google Analytics 4 measurement ID, if loaded directly instead of through GTM.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GA4_ID=

# Meta (Facebook) Pixel ID.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_META_PIXEL_ID=

# Google Search Console verification token. Renders a <meta> tag and sets no
# cookie, so it is outside the consent gate.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GSC_VERIFICATION=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Forms

# Google reCAPTCHA v3 site key. The script loads lazily, on the first submit.
# Set this together with RECAPTCHA_SECRET on the API or leave both empty — see
# docs/deployment.md. Either one alone silently breaks every form: without the
# site key the API rejects every submission, and without the secret Google is
# contacted for nothing.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_RECAPTCHA_SITE_KEY=
